-- Q1: medium / L2 — Budgetary entry with surplus
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Willowdale adopts a general fund budget for fiscal year 2027 with estimated revenues of $24,600,000 and appropriations of $23,900,000. Under GASB standards, what entry records the adopted budget?', '["Debit Estimated Revenues $24,600,000; credit Appropriations $23,900,000 and credit Budgetary Fund Balance $700,000", "Debit Estimated Revenues $24,600,000; credit Appropriations $24,600,000", "Debit Appropriations $23,900,000 and debit Budgetary Fund Balance $700,000; credit Estimated Revenues $24,600,000", "Debit Cash $24,600,000; credit Budgetary Fund Balance $24,600,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 and traditional budgetary accounting, governments record the adopted budget by debiting Estimated Revenues, crediting Appropriations, and plugging the difference to Budgetary Fund Balance. Because estimated revenues ($24,600,000) exceed appropriations ($23,900,000), the $700,000 difference is credited to Budgetary Fund Balance, reflecting an anticipated surplus. Choice C reverses the debit/credit structure, which would incorrectly record the budget as if appropriations exceeded revenues.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Derived tax revenue recognition with availability
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The State of Kensington collects a 6% retail sales tax. During fiscal year 2026, taxable sales of $720,000,000 occur. The state collects $41,800,000 by December 31 and receives $1,400,000 in January within the 60-day availability period. Under modified accrual, what sales tax revenue should the general fund recognize?', '["$41,800,000", "$43,200,000", "$42,000,000", "$1,400,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 33, sales taxes are derived tax revenues recognized when the underlying exchange occurs, subject to the availability criterion for governmental funds. Revenue that is measurable and collected within the 60-day availability period ($41,800,000 + $1,400,000 = $43,200,000) is recognized. Choice A excludes the January collections that meet the availability test, understating revenue by $1,400,000.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Purchases method vs consumption method for supplies
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Thornberry''s general fund purchases $540,000 of office supplies on April 1, 2026. By December 31, $390,000 has been consumed, leaving $150,000 in the supply room. The city uses the purchases method. The finance director asks how switching to the consumption method would affect reported expenditures. What is the most likely effect of adopting the consumption method?', '["Expenditures decrease by $150,000 because only consumed supplies are recognized as expenditures", "Expenditures increase by $150,000 because inventory carrying costs are added", "Expenditures remain unchanged because both methods produce the same year-end result", "Expenditures decrease by $390,000 because supplies are capitalized until consumed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, the purchases method records the full $540,000 as expenditures at acquisition. The consumption method records only $390,000 (consumed) as expenditures and reports the $150,000 remaining as inventory on the balance sheet, reducing expenditures by $150,000. Choice C is incorrect because the two methods produce different expenditure amounts whenever ending inventory differs from beginning inventory.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — GASB 65 bond issuance costs treatment
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Maplebrook issues $14,000,000 in general obligation bonds through its capital projects fund, paying $165,000 in underwriter fees and $42,000 in legal costs at closing. The finance director initially capitalizes the $207,000 as a deferred charge on the government-wide statement of net position. Under GASB Statement No. 65, what is the most likely effect of properly reporting these issuance costs?', '["The $207,000 is recognized as an expense in the period incurred on the government-wide statements, reducing net position", "The $207,000 is amortized over the bond term as a component of interest expense", "The $207,000 is netted against the bond proceeds and reduces the face amount reported", "The $207,000 is reported as a deferred outflow of resources and amortized over the bond life"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 65, bond issuance costs (except insurance) must be recognized as an expense in the period incurred, not capitalized or amortized. This reversed the prior practice of deferring and amortizing these costs. The $207,000 reduces net position immediately. Choice B reflects the pre-GASB 65 treatment that is no longer permitted; issuance costs are not interest expense components under current standards.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Encumbrance lapsing at year-end
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Havenbrook''s general fund has $275,000 in outstanding encumbrances for park equipment at fiscal year-end. The city''s policy treats encumbrances as lapsing. The equipment is delivered in January of the following year for $282,000. Under governmental fund accounting, how should the general fund report the encumbrance at year-end?', '["Reverse the $275,000 encumbrance entry and disclose in the notes; record the $282,000 as an expenditure in the new fiscal year", "Report $275,000 as committed fund balance and record the $7,000 excess as an expenditure in the new year", "Record $275,000 as an expenditure in the current year and $7,000 in the new year", "Report $275,000 as a liability on the general fund balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB standards, when encumbrances lapse at year-end, the outstanding encumbrance entry is reversed and the commitment is disclosed in the notes. In the new fiscal year, the full $282,000 invoice is charged as an expenditure against the new year''s appropriations. Choice B describes nonlapsing treatment, where the encumbered amount would be reported as committed or assigned fund balance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Expenditure vs expense distinction in governmental accounting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, how do expenditures reported in governmental fund statements differ from expenses reported in government-wide statements?', '["Expenditures measure current financial resource outflows when liabilities are incurred, while expenses include depreciation and other accrual-based costs", "Expenditures and expenses are identical terms used interchangeably across both reporting perspectives", "Expenditures include depreciation, while expenses exclude long-term asset consumption", "Expenditures are reported on a cash basis, while expenses are reported on a modified accrual basis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus and report expenditures when liabilities are incurred (e.g., capital outlay is fully expensed at purchase). Government-wide statements use the economic resources focus and report expenses under full accrual, including depreciation. Choice B is incorrect because the two terms reflect fundamentally different measurement focuses.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Definition of current financial resources measurement focus
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, what does the current financial resources measurement focus report?', '["Resources available to spend in the near term, excluding long-term assets and long-term liabilities", "All economic resources including capital assets and long-term debt", "Only cash and cash equivalents on hand at year-end", "Net position classified as restricted, unrestricted, and invested in capital assets"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, the current financial resources measurement focus used by governmental funds reports resources available to spend in the near term. Long-term assets (capital assets) and long-term liabilities (bonds payable) are excluded from governmental fund balance sheets. Choice B describes the economic resources measurement focus used by proprietary funds and government-wide statements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — ISF elimination effect on government-wide expenses
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Cedarfield operates a printing services internal service fund that bills governmental departments $1,800,000 during fiscal year 2026. The fund''s actual costs are $1,650,000, producing a $150,000 surplus. When preparing the government-wide statements, the finance director must consolidate the internal service fund into governmental activities. What is the most likely effect on total governmental activities expenses?', '["Total expenses decrease by $150,000 because the markup over actual cost is eliminated", "Total expenses increase by $1,800,000 because interfund billings are added to department costs", "Total expenses remain unchanged because internal service fund operations are excluded entirely", "Total expenses decrease by $1,800,000 because all interfund charges are eliminated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, internal service funds are typically consolidated into governmental activities in the government-wide statements. The interfund billings ($1,800,000) and the fund''s costs ($1,650,000) are netted, and only the actual costs flow through. The $150,000 surplus (markup above cost) is eliminated, reducing total governmental activities expenses. Choice D is incorrect because only the excess charge is eliminated, not the underlying service costs.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Capital asset threshold policy application
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Ridgecreek has a capital asset capitalization threshold of $10,000 for equipment. During fiscal year 2026, the town purchases eight identical desktop computers at $4,500 each ($36,000 total) for the police department. Each computer operates independently. The finance director asks whether the computers should be capitalized. Under GASB 34, what is the most likely reporting treatment in the government-wide statements?', '["Expense the full $36,000 because each individual unit is below the $10,000 threshold", "Capitalize the $36,000 as a single asset because the computers serve a common function", "Capitalize $36,000 and depreciate over the computers'' useful life because the aggregate exceeds $10,000", "Report $36,000 as an other financing use in the governmental fund statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, capitalization thresholds are applied to individual assets, not aggregate purchases. Because each $4,500 computer is below the $10,000 threshold and each operates independently, none qualifies for capitalization. The full $36,000 is expensed on the government-wide statements. Choice C incorrectly aggregates the purchase; GASB applies thresholds per unit unless the items form a network or system.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluate ISF reclassification when external billings grow
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Graystone operates an internal service fund for vehicle maintenance that historically served only county departments. Over the past three years, external billings to neighboring municipalities have grown from 8% to 35% of total revenue. County departments account for the remaining 65%. The external auditor questions whether the fund should be reclassified. Evaluate the most appropriate fund classification under GASB 34.', '["Reclassify as an enterprise fund because external users now receive a significant portion of services, meeting the criteria for enterprise fund reporting", "Retain as an internal service fund because county departments remain the predominant customer at 65%", "Reclassify as a special revenue fund because the external revenue represents a restricted revenue source", "Retain as an internal service fund but disclose the external billings as a contingency in the notes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, enterprise funds are used when the predominant customer group is external to the government or when the government has elected enterprise fund reporting for an activity that charges fees. With 35% external revenue growing steadily, the activity increasingly resembles a business-type activity. GASB guidance suggests reclassification when the predominant customer shifts or external charges become significant. Choice B is plausible but ignores the trend and the activity''s evolving character; a 35% external share with upward trajectory warrants reclassification.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Notes as integral part of basic financial statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, how are notes to the financial statements classified within a government''s financial report?', '["As an integral part of the basic financial statements", "As required supplementary information presented after the financial statements", "As supplementary information included in the statistical section", "As optional disclosures at management''s discretion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, notes to the financial statements are an integral part of the basic financial statements, not supplementary information. Notes provide essential disclosures about accounting policies, capital assets, long-term debt, pensions, and other items necessary for understanding the statements. Choice B incorrectly classifies notes as RSI; MD&A and budgetary comparisons are RSI, but notes are part of the basic statements themselves.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Proprietary fund required financial statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, which three financial statements must proprietary funds present?', '["Statement of net position, statement of revenues/expenses/changes in fund net position, and statement of cash flows", "Balance sheet, income statement, and statement of retained earnings", "Statement of net position, statement of activities, and budgetary comparison schedule", "Statement of fiduciary net position, statement of changes in fiduciary net position, and notes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, proprietary funds (enterprise and internal service) present three financial statements: a statement of net position, a statement of revenues, expenses, and changes in fund net position, and a statement of cash flows. Choice B uses commercial accounting terminology (income statement, retained earnings) that does not apply to government proprietary funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Budgetary closing entries and effect on fund balance
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Greenleaf''s general fund records estimated revenues of $15,200,000 and appropriations of $14,800,000 at the start of fiscal year 2026. Actual revenues are $15,500,000 and actual expenditures are $15,100,000. At year-end, the finance director must close all budgetary and actual accounts. What is the most likely effect of the closing process on the general fund balance?', '["Fund balance increases by $400,000, the difference between actual revenues and actual expenditures", "Fund balance increases by $700,000 because both the budgetary surplus and actual surplus are added", "Fund balance remains unchanged because budgetary and actual entries offset each other", "Fund balance increases by $300,000, the difference between estimated revenues and actual expenditures"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB budgetary accounting, the closing process reverses the budgetary entry (debit Appropriations, credit Estimated Revenues, plug Budgetary Fund Balance) and closes actual revenue and expenditure accounts to fund balance. The net effect on fund balance is the actual surplus: $15,500,000 revenues minus $15,100,000 expenditures = $400,000 increase. Choice B incorrectly double-counts the budgetary surplus; the budgetary entry nets to zero upon closing.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Program revenue identification on statement of activities
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Kenworth reports the following in its government-wide statement of activities: property taxes $18,500,000, a federal transit operating grant $3,200,000, municipal parking fees $890,000, unrestricted state aid $4,100,000, and investment income $620,000. Under GASB 34, what is the total amount of program revenues?', '["$4,090,000", "$27,310,000", "$22,690,000", "$8,810,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, program revenues include charges for services and grants/contributions directly attributable to specific functions. The federal transit grant ($3,200,000) and parking fees ($890,000) are program revenues, totaling $4,090,000. Property taxes, unrestricted state aid, and investment income are general revenues. Choice D incorrectly includes state aid and investment income as program revenues, but unrestricted revenues are classified as general revenues.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Modified accrual compensated absences recognition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Pinewood''s general fund accrues $2,400,000 in compensated absence liabilities for accumulated employee vacation pay at December 31, 2026. Of this amount, $680,000 is expected to be paid from current resources within the first quarter of 2027, and the remaining $1,720,000 will be paid in subsequent periods. Under modified accrual, what is the most likely effect on the general fund expenditures?', '["Recognize $680,000 as an expenditure because only the amount payable from current available resources is recorded in the governmental fund", "Recognize $2,400,000 as an expenditure because the full liability has been incurred", "Recognize no expenditure because compensated absences are long-term liabilities excluded from governmental funds", "Recognize $1,720,000 as an expenditure and defer the $680,000 current portion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under modified accrual accounting (GASB 34), compensated absences in governmental funds are recognized as expenditures only to the extent they will be liquidated with expendable available financial resources. The $680,000 expected to be paid from current resources is recognized; the remaining $1,720,000 is reported only in the government-wide statements as a long-term liability. Choice B applies full accrual treatment, which is inappropriate for governmental funds.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — GASB 54 assigned fund balance authority
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, who may establish assigned fund balance in a governmental fund?', '["The governing body or a body or official authorized by the governing body", "Only the state legislature through enabling legislation", "Only external grantors or creditors with legal claims on the resources", "The independent auditor during the annual financial statement audit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, assigned fund balance reflects an intended use of resources that is neither restricted nor committed. The governing body may delegate the authority to assign fund balance to a finance director or other official. Choice B describes restricted classification (external constraints), not assigned classification which involves internal intent without formal action at the highest level.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — GASB 77 tax abatement as revenue reduction
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Greendale enters a 10-year property tax abatement with Silverline Industries, reducing Silverline''s annual taxes by $480,000 to incentivize construction of a distribution center. The town''s total property tax levy is $36,000,000. Under GASB Statement No. 77, how should the town report the abatement in its financial statements?', '["Reduce property tax revenue by $480,000 and disclose the abatement program in the notes, including purpose, duration, and dollar amount", "Record a $480,000 expenditure for economic development and maintain full revenue recognition", "Report $480,000 as a deferred outflow of resources amortized over the 10-year abatement period", "Disclose the abatement only in the statistical section with no effect on the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 77, tax abatements are reported as reductions of tax revenue, not as expenditures. The town must also disclose the abatement program''s purpose, terms, dollar amount, and commitments. Choice B incorrectly treats the foregone tax as an expenditure, which would overstate both revenues and expenditures and misrepresent the nature of the transaction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — GASB 84 custodial fund reporting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Maplewood County collects $3,800,000 in property taxes on behalf of three independent school districts during fiscal year 2026 and remits the collections monthly. The county retains a 1.5% administrative fee. Under GASB Statement No. 84, in which fund type should the county report the tax collections held for the school districts?', '["Custodial fund", "Agency fund", "Special revenue fund", "Private-purpose trust fund"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 84, pass-through tax collections held for other governments are reported in custodial funds. GASB 84 eliminated the agency fund classification, replacing it with custodial funds that now report a statement of changes in fiduciary net position. Choice B references the pre-GASB 84 agency fund designation, which is no longer a valid fund type under current standards.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate special-purpose government reporting options
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Riverdale Public Library District is a special-purpose government that provides only library services, funded by a dedicated property tax levy and state aid. It has no business-type activities and one governmental program. The board is considering which financial reporting model to adopt. Under GASB 34, evaluate the most appropriate reporting approach for this entity.', '["Present only governmental fund financial statements combined with government-wide data in a single set of statements, because the district is a special-purpose government engaged in a single governmental activity", "Present full dual-perspective statements (government-wide and fund) because all governments must use the standard GASB 34 model", "Present only proprietary fund statements using full accrual because library fees constitute exchange transactions", "Present only fiduciary fund statements because the library holds resources on behalf of the public"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 135-138, a special-purpose government engaged in a single governmental activity may combine government-wide and fund data into a single set of financial statements, avoiding the full dual-perspective model. This streamlined approach is specifically designed for entities like library districts. Choice B is incorrect because GASB 34 provides this explicit exception for single-activity special-purpose governments.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Evaluate GASB 46 enabling legislation and restricted net position
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The State of Eastfield enacted legislation imposing a 2% lodging tax with proceeds dedicated exclusively to tourism promotion. The legislature holds the power to repeal the statute. At fiscal year-end, $4,800,000 in unspent lodging tax collections remain. The finance director classifies the amount as unrestricted net position, reasoning the legislature could redirect the funds at any time. Evaluate the finance director''s classification under GASB 46 and GASB 34.', '["The classification is incorrect; the $4,800,000 should be restricted because enabling legislation creates an external restriction that remains in force until the legislation is amended or repealed", "The classification is correct; legislative authority to repeal the statute means no enforceable restriction exists", "The $4,800,000 should be committed because the legislature is the highest level of decision-making authority", "The $4,800,000 should be assigned because the dedication is an expressed intent without legal enforcement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 46, resources constrained by enabling legislation are classified as restricted net position, not unrestricted. The restriction remains legally enforceable as long as the enabling legislation is in effect, regardless of the legislature''s theoretical ability to repeal it. Choice B is the finance director''s flawed reasoning; GASB 46 explicitly states that the possibility of future repeal does not negate the current restriction.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Exchange vs nonexchange transaction distinction
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, how does an exchange transaction differ from a nonexchange transaction?', '["In an exchange transaction, each party gives and receives approximately equal value; in a nonexchange transaction, the government receives value without giving equal value in return", "Exchange transactions involve cash payments while nonexchange transactions are always noncash", "Exchange transactions are recorded on the accrual basis while nonexchange transactions use the cash basis", "Exchange transactions occur between governments while nonexchange transactions occur between a government and private parties"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 33, exchange transactions involve a reciprocal transfer of approximately equal value between parties (e.g., water service for user fees). Nonexchange transactions involve the government receiving value without directly giving equal value in return (e.g., taxes, grants, fines). Choice D is incorrect because both exchange and nonexchange transactions can occur between any combination of governments and private parties.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — GASB 68 post-measurement date contribution as deferred outflow
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Norwood makes a $1,950,000 pension contribution on May 15, 2026, for its fiscal year ending June 30, 2026. The pension plan''s measurement date is March 31, 2026. Under GASB Statement No. 68, how should the city report the $1,950,000 contribution on the government-wide financial statements?', '["As a deferred outflow of resources, to be recognized as a reduction of net pension liability in the following measurement period", "As pension expense for the current fiscal year because the contribution was made before the reporting date", "As a prepaid asset on the statement of net position, amortized over the employees'' remaining service life", "As a reduction of the net pension liability on the measurement date because the cash was paid during the reporting period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 68, employer contributions made after the measurement date but before the reporting date are reported as deferred outflows of resources. The $1,950,000 is not recognized as expense or as a liability reduction until the next measurement period. Choice B is incorrect because GASB 68 ties expense recognition to the measurement date, not the contribution date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Infrastructure asset definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, which of the following is an example of a major infrastructure asset that governments must report?', '["Roads, bridges, and water distribution systems", "Office furniture and computer equipment", "Land held for resale by an enterprise fund", "Investments in certificates of deposit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, infrastructure assets are long-lived capital assets that are stationary in nature and normally can be preserved for a significantly greater number of years than most capital assets. Examples include roads, bridges, tunnels, drainage systems, and water/sewer systems. Choice B describes general capital assets (equipment), not infrastructure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Evaluate modified approach eligibility when conditions deteriorate
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Millfield County uses the modified approach under GASB 34 for its 1,800-mile road network. The latest condition assessment reveals 24% of roads below the county''s established condition target of 80% acceptable. Annual preservation spending was $5,200,000 against an engineering estimate of $7,800,000 needed to maintain the target. The finance director argues the county can continue the modified approach because the assessment was completed on schedule. Evaluate the finance director''s position.', '["The position is flawed; the county must likely revert to the depreciation approach because roads are not being maintained at the established condition level, a prerequisite for the modified approach", "The position is correct; completing timely condition assessments is the only requirement for continuing the modified approach", "The position is correct as long as the county discloses the condition shortfall in the notes to the financial statements", "The position is flawed; the county must capitalize the $2,600,000 spending shortfall as a deferred maintenance liability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, the modified approach requires both timely condition assessments and maintaining the asset at or above the government''s established condition level. With 24% below the 80% target and preservation spending $2,600,000 below the needed amount, the condition requirement is not met. The county must revert to depreciation. Choice B is incorrect because timely assessment alone is insufficient; maintaining the established condition level is equally mandatory.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Reconciliation adjustment for prepaid items and inventory
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ashworth''s general fund reports $320,000 in prepaid insurance and $480,000 in supplies inventory at year-end. The city uses the purchases method for supplies. In reconciling from governmental fund statements to government-wide statements, what is the most likely adjustment for these items?', '["No adjustment is needed for prepaids or inventory because they appear on both the fund and government-wide balance sheets under the current financial resources focus", "Add $480,000 to net position because supplies inventory reported under the purchases method was expensed in the fund but represents a consumable asset on the government-wide statements", "Subtract $800,000 from net position because prepaids and inventory are not economic resources", "Add $320,000 for prepaids and subtract $480,000 for inventory, producing a net decrease of $160,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, when the purchases method is used, supplies are expensed at acquisition in the fund statements. In the reconciliation to government-wide statements, the $480,000 inventory must be added back because it represents a consumable asset under the economic resources focus. Prepaids are already reported on the fund balance sheet (with a nonspendable fund balance offset), so no adjustment is needed for them. Choice A is incorrect because the purchases method creates a difference requiring reconciliation for inventory.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Permanent fund principal and earnings reporting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Heathrow operates a permanent fund holding a $4,000,000 endowment. The agreement requires the principal to remain intact, with only investment earnings supporting county library programs. During fiscal year 2026, the fund earns $245,000 in interest and dividends. Under GASB standards, how should the permanent fund report the principal and earnings?', '["Report $4,000,000 as nonspendable fund balance and recognize $245,000 as restricted revenue for library programs", "Report $4,245,000 as restricted fund balance because both principal and earnings serve the library purpose", "Report $4,000,000 as committed fund balance and $245,000 as unassigned fund balance", "Report $4,000,000 as a long-term liability and $245,000 as other financing sources"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, the $4,000,000 principal is classified as nonspendable fund balance because its form (legally required to remain intact) prevents spending. The $245,000 in earnings is recognized as revenue and classified as restricted fund balance because external constraints limit its use to library programs. Choice B incorrectly merges principal and earnings into one classification, ignoring the nonspendable constraint on principal.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluate GASB 10 premium charges reasonableness
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Thornbury operates an internal service fund for health insurance self-coverage. Over the past four years, premiums charged to departments have exceeded total claims (including incurred-but-not-reported) by an average of $1,100,000 annually. Accumulated net position has grown to $5,200,000 against average annual claims of $6,800,000. The external auditor questions whether the premium structure is appropriate. Under GASB Statement No. 10, evaluate the most appropriate conclusion.', '["The premium charges appear excessive; the accumulated surplus significantly exceeds a reasonable reserve, and premiums should be reduced to approximate the expected cost of claims to avoid distorting departmental cost allocations", "The premium charges are appropriate because building reserves is a prudent risk management strategy with no upper limit", "The accumulated surplus must be returned to the general fund as a nonreciprocal transfer", "The premium charges are irrelevant because internal service fund surpluses are eliminated in the government-wide statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 10, internal service fund charges should approximate the cost of providing coverage, including a reasonable provision for expected future catastrophic losses. An accumulated surplus of $5,200,000 (76% of annual claims) growing by $1,100,000 annually suggests charges materially exceed costs, distorting departmental expense allocations. Choice B ignores GASB 10''s requirement that charges approximate cost; unlimited reserve accumulation is not supported.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Two activity categories in government-wide statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, what are the two categories of activities presented in the government-wide financial statements?', '["Governmental activities and business-type activities", "Operating activities and nonoperating activities", "General fund activities and special fund activities", "Fiduciary activities and nonfiduciary activities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, government-wide financial statements present two columns: governmental activities (funded primarily by taxes and intergovernmental revenues) and business-type activities (funded primarily by user charges). Fiduciary activities are excluded entirely. Choice D is incorrect because fiduciary funds are not reported in the government-wide statements and do not form a separate activity category.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L2 — GASB 33 imposed nonexchange — fines classification
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Redbrook''s code enforcement division assesses $425,000 in zoning violation fines during fiscal year 2026 and collects $365,000 by December 31. An additional $35,000 is expected within 60 days. Under GASB Statement No. 33, how should these fines be classified for revenue recognition?', '["As imposed nonexchange revenues, with $400,000 recognized as revenue under modified accrual", "As derived tax revenues, with $425,000 recognized when assessed", "As voluntary nonexchange transactions, with $365,000 recognized when collected", "As exchange transactions, with $425,000 recognized when the fines are levied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 33, fines and penalties are classified as imposed nonexchange revenues, recognized when an enforceable legal claim arises (assessment), subject to the availability criterion. Revenue equals collections ($365,000) plus amounts available within 60 days ($35,000) = $400,000. The remaining $25,000 is reported as deferred inflows. Choice B is incorrect because derived tax revenues arise from underlying exchange transactions (sales, income), not penalties.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — GASB 54 stabilization arrangement classification
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Millcreek''s council enacts an ordinance establishing a $6,000,000 stabilization (rainy-day) reserve in the general fund, requiring a three-fourths council vote to authorize spending. The ordinance specifies the reserve may be used only when actual revenues fall at least 5% below budgeted revenues. State law does not mandate the arrangement. The finance director classifies the $6,000,000 as restricted fund balance because the ordinance limits its use. Under GASB 54, what is the most likely correct classification?', '["Committed fund balance, because the governing body''s formal action at the highest level of authority establishes the constraint, not an external party", "Restricted fund balance, because the three-fourths vote requirement and revenue-decline trigger impose a legal restriction", "Assigned fund balance, because stabilization reserves represent intended uses without formal commitment", "Unassigned fund balance, because internal reserves do not meet any constraint criteria"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, stabilization arrangements established by the governing body through formal action (ordinance with supermajority release requirement) are classified as committed fund balance. Restricted classification requires constraints imposed by external parties (creditors, grantors, other governments) or enabling legislation, which an internal ordinance is not. Choice B is incorrect because the city council''s own ordinance is an internal constraint, not an external restriction.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
